<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "pwm.fan.control">
<!ENTITY author    "unknown">
<!ENTITY version   "2026.11.17">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://git.int.idmedia.no/idmedia/unraid/pwm-fan-control/raw/branch/main/pwm.fan.control.plg">
<!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
]>
<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" min="6.12.0" icon="snowflake-o" launch="Settings/PWMFanControl">

<CHANGES>
###2026.11.17
- Speed test: measures the RPM of a fan from 100% down to 0%, shows where it stops and the speed it needs to start again
- Warns when a group's min speed is below the speed one of its fans needs to start
- Warns when a fan stops spinning (0 RPM) while it should run, and when another program changes a fan's speed
- Problems are sent as Unraid alert notifications when they last more than a minute, a crash or watchdog restart right away, and a notice when resolved
- Warns when a fan runs much faster or slower than its speed test measured, which usually means fans were moved to other headers
- Missing fans say why: the chip is gone (driver not loaded, ACPI conflict) or only that output (hardware or BIOS changed)
- Hardware check on the settings page: fan driver, fan outputs, ACPI resource conflicts and whether acpi_enforce_resources=lax is needed
- New setting "Drivers to load": loads the fan controller driver (with options) at boot
- Points out fans in a group without a paired RPM sensor, which can't be watched
- A single failed temperature reading no longer sends fans to full speed; two in a row still do
- An error in the service, or a crash, now runs the fans at full speed instead of leaving them at their last speed
- Disk temperature sensors of the kernel (drivetemp) are no longer read, so disks can spin down
- Dashboard tile uses a lighter request and shows warnings
- Shows when a fan header is in DC mode
- Settings and fan names are only written to the flash drive when they change

###2026.11.16
- First release of PWM Fan Control, replacing the unreleased Array Fan Control plugin
- Fan groups: every group follows one or more temperature sources, the highest speed wins
- Temperature sources: array disks, Nvidia GPUs (nvidia-smi) and kernel sensors (CPU, motherboard, NVMe)
- Background service checking every 2-30 seconds, with a watchdog and a slow-down delay
- Runs at full speed when a temperature can't be read
- Identify fans from the web UI with live RPM readings, name fans and sensors
- Fan curve and 24 hour history charts per group, dashboard tile
- Imports settings from Array Fan Control
</CHANGES>

<!-- Remove files from a previous version -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/pwm.fan.control
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="&emhttp;/PWMFanControl.page">
<INLINE><![CDATA[Menu="Utilities"
Title="PWM Fan Control"
Icon="fa-snowflake-o"
Tag="snowflake-o"
Markdown="false"
---
<?php
$pfc_dir = '/plugins/pwm.fan.control';
$pfc_csrf = $var['csrf_token'] ?? (@parse_ini_file('/var/local/emhttp/var.ini')['csrf_token'] ?? '');
$pfc_v = @filemtime("/usr/local/emhttp$pfc_dir/js/pfc.js");
?>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<?=$pfc_dir?>/css/pfc.css?v=<?=$pfc_v?>">

<div id="pfc" data-api="<?=$pfc_dir?>/include/api.php" data-csrf="<?=htmlspecialchars($pfc_csrf)?>">

  <div id="pfc-banners"></div>

  <div class="pfc-applybar pfc-hidden" id="pfc-applybar">
    <span><i class="fa fa-pencil"></i> You have unsaved changes.</span>
    <button type="button" class="pfc-apply">Apply</button>
    <button type="button" class="pfc-reset">Discard</button>
  </div>

  <!-- Status -->
  <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-tachometer title"></i>Status</span></div>
  <div class="pfc-status" id="pfc-status"></div>

  <!-- Groups -->
  <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-object-group title"></i>Fan groups</span></div>
  <p class="pfc-muted">
    A group is a set of fans that follow one or more temperature sources. Every source has its own curve, and the group
    runs at the <b>highest speed any of its sources asks for</b>. Fans speed up right away and slow down only after the
    lower speed has been needed for the <b>slow-down delay</b>. If a source can't be read, the group runs at full speed.
  </p>
  <div id="pfc-groups"></div>
  <p><button type="button" id="pfc-add-group"><i class="fa fa-plus"></i> Add group</button></p>

  <!-- Fans -->
  <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-snowflake-o title"></i>Fans</span></div>
  <p class="pfc-muted">
    Click <b>Identify</b> to slow a fan down and spin it up to full speed, then watch or listen to see which fan reacts.
    The RPM sensor that reacts is paired with the output automatically. Give each fan a name, then add it to a group above.
    <b>Speed test</b> measures the RPM from 100% down to 0%, and shows the speed a fan stops at and the speed it needs to
    start again. With a paired RPM sensor, a fan that stops spinning while it should run is reported, and after a speed test also
    a fan that runs much faster or slower than measured, which usually means fans were moved to other headers.
  </p>
  <div class="pfc-table-wrap">
    <table class="pfc-table" id="pfc-pwm-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Output</th><th>Speed</th><th>Current setting</th><th>RPM sensor</th><th>Group</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody><tr><td colspan="7" class="pfc-muted">Loading...</td></tr></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div id="pfc-fantest" class="pfc-fantest pfc-hidden"></div>

  <div id="pfc-rpm-section" class="pfc-hidden">
    <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-circle-o-notch title"></i>Other fan sensors</span></div>
    <p class="pfc-muted">RPM sensors that are not paired with a controllable output, e.g. fans on headers without PWM control. You can still give them a name.</p>
    <div class="pfc-table-wrap">
      <table class="pfc-table" id="pfc-rpm-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Sensor</th><th>Speed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Sources -->
  <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-thermometer-half title"></i>Temperature sources</span></div>
  <p class="pfc-muted">Everything that reports a temperature. Give sources a name to recognise them in groups and charts.
    <span id="pfc-nvidia-note"></span></p>
  <div class="pfc-table-wrap">
    <table class="pfc-table" id="pfc-source-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Source</th><th>Temperature</th><th>Used by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <label class="pfc-muted"><input type="checkbox" id="pfc-show-all-sensors"> Show all kernel sensors</label>

  <!-- Disks -->
  <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-hdd-o title"></i>Array disks source</span></div>
  <p class="pfc-muted">The <b>Array disks</b> source reports the hottest monitored disk that is spun up. Untick a disk to ignore it,
    e.g. an SSD that runs hot but isn't cooled by these fans. Unraid only refreshes disk temperatures every
    30 minutes by default. You can lower this under Settings &gt; Disk Settings &gt; Tunable (poll_attributes).</p>
  <div class="pfc-table-wrap">
    <table class="pfc-table" id="pfc-disk-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Disk</th><th>Type</th><th>State</th><th>Temperature</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <form class="pfc-form" onsubmit="return false">
    <dl><dt>Disk types to monitor:</dt><dd class="pfc-checks">
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-cfg="array.include.Parity"> Parity</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-cfg="array.include.Data"> Data</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-cfg="array.include.Cache"> Pools/cache</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-cfg="array.include.Unassigned"> Unassigned</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-cfg="array.include.Flash"> Flash</label>
    </dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>Full speed during parity check:</dt><dd>
      <select data-cfg="array.parity_full_speed" data-type="bool"><option value="true">Yes</option><option value="false">No</option></select>
    </dd></dl>
  </form>

  <!-- Settings -->
  <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-cog title"></i>Settings</span></div>
  <form class="pfc-form" onsubmit="return false">
    <dl><dt>Enable fan control:</dt><dd>
      <select data-cfg="enabled" data-type="bool"><option value="false">No</option><option value="true">Yes</option></select>
      <span class="pfc-muted">While disabled, the plugin never changes any fan speed.</span>
    </dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>Check temperatures every:</dt><dd>
      <select data-cfg="interval" data-type="int">
        <option value="2">2 seconds</option><option value="3">3 seconds</option><option value="5">5 seconds</option>
        <option value="10">10 seconds</option><option value="15">15 seconds</option><option value="30">30 seconds</option>
      </select>
    </dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>Log speed changes to syslog:</dt><dd>
      <select data-cfg="log_changes" data-type="bool"><option value="true">Yes</option><option value="false">No</option></select>
      <span class="pfc-muted">Changes of 5% or more, and every switch to or from full speed.</span>
    </dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>Send notifications:</dt><dd>
      <select data-cfg="notify" data-type="bool"><option value="true">Yes</option><option value="false">No</option></select>
      <span class="pfc-muted">An Unraid alert when a problem lasts more than a minute (a fan stopped or was moved, a fan or source is missing,
        the service failed, ...) and a notice when it is resolved.</span>
    </dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>Drivers to load:</dt><dd>
      <input type="text" class="pfc-wide" data-cfg="drivers" maxlength="200" placeholder="e.g. nct6775">
      <span class="pfc-muted">Fan controller drivers loaded at boot, before fan control starts. Separate several with ";", options are allowed
        (e.g. "it87 force_id=0x8628"). Leave empty if another plugin (e.g. Dynamix System Temperature) loads the driver.</span>
    </dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>Identify: slow speed:</dt><dd><input type="number" min="0" max="100" class="pfc-narrow" data-cfg="identify.low_pwm" data-type="pct"> % <span class="pfc-muted">0% stops most fans.</span></dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>Identify: seconds per step:</dt><dd><input type="number" min="3" max="30" class="pfc-narrow" data-cfg="identify.seconds" data-type="int"></dd></dl>
    <dl><dt>&nbsp;</dt><dd>
      <button type="button" class="pfc-apply">Apply</button>
      <button type="button" class="pfc-reset">Discard</button>
    </dd></dl>
  </form>

  <!-- Hardware check -->
  <div class="title"><span class="left"><i class="fa fa-stethoscope title"></i>Hardware check</span></div>
  <p class="pfc-muted">What fan control needs from the system. <b>acpi_enforce_resources=lax</b> is only needed when the kernel log shows an
    ACPI resource conflict for the fan chip: then the BIOS claims the chip and the driver can't load or offers no fan outputs.</p>
  <div class="pfc-table-wrap">
    <table class="pfc-table" id="pfc-hw-table"><tbody></tbody></table>
  </div>
</div>

<script src="<?=$pfc_dir?>/js/pfc.js?v=<?=$pfc_v?>"></script>
]]></INLINE>
